# Who must approve a change to what.
#
# `master` requires one approving review. Without this file that review can come from
# anyone, including someone who has never read the code being changed.
#
# Two effects, which are easy to confuse:
#   1. Once this file is on the DEFAULT BRANCH, GitHub automatically requests a review
#      from the matching owner on every PR touching their paths. No setting required --
#      it starts happening as soon as the file reaches `master`.
#   2. Turning on "Require review from Code Owners" in the ruleset makes that approval
#      mandatory rather than merely requested.
#
# So merging this to `dev` does nothing; it has to reach `master` before effect 1 begins.
#
# An owner who lacks write access to the repository is ignored silently -- no error, the
# rule simply never matches. Check handles before relying on a line.
#
# Last matching rule wins, so the specific paths at the bottom override the default.

# Default owner for anything not claimed below.
*                       @Potaeeatsthis

# Verification harness, CI, and the deployment configuration.
/scripts/               @KLAharris
/.github/               @KLAharris
/.claude/               @KLAharris

# The engine and the interface need no rules of their own: the default above already
# assigns them, and the commit history agrees -- @Potaeeatsthis authors the large majority
# of wasm/src/, app/, components/, lib/ and stores/. An explicit rule that names the same
# owner as the default is pure noise, and "last matching rule wins" makes duplicated rules
# a hazard rather than documentation.
#
# Add a path here only when its owner DIFFERS from the default, which is what the three
# rules above are doing.
